{"title":"Relatively prime","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Positive\">positive</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Integer\" title=\"Integer\">integers</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are said to be <b>relatively prime</b> or <b>coprime</b> if they share no <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Common_divisor\" title=\"Common divisor\"> common divisors</a> greater than 1. That is, their <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Greatest_common_divisor\" title=\"Greatest common divisor\">greatest common divisor</a> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/5/78535d0b3120e26aa7ebfc02aeae28e5f0809201.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m, n) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />.  Equivalently, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> must have no <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime\">prime</a> divisors in common.  The positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27f583697295fc6f723862c13228ee55107c33ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"34\" /> is in lowest terms.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Number_Theory\">Number Theory</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Number Theory\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Relatively prime numbers show up frequently in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_theory\" title=\"Number theory\">number theory</a> formulas and derivations:\n</p><p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function\" title=\"Euler&#39;s totient function\">Euler's totient function</a> determines the number of positive integers less than any given positive integer that is relatively prime to that number.\n</p><p>Consecutive positive integers are always relatively prime, since, if a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> divides both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />, then it must divide their difference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75cfd592349a2b956d43174740d14e634873863.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1)-n = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />, which is impossible since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/8/fc86688cd9e5a4ce262df69345bacac9f89f8ed4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Two integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> are relatively prime if and only if there exist some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/9/8396bfe1ef3285ddcf50f894609574d30fdc7384.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y\\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/8/548b4593bc88cee312797a1016548c2a698ccf5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ax+by=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"92\" height=\"16\" /> (a special case of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bezout%27s_Lemma\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Bezout&#39;s Lemma\">Bezout's Lemma</a>). The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euclidean_Algorithm\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Euclidean Algorithm\">Euclidean Algorithm</a> can be used to compute the coefficients <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/4/b6400c6fe3f1ed70e5afd387a9686cdc3fa09531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" />.\n</p><p>For two relatively prime numbers, their <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Least_common_multiple\" title=\"Least common multiple\">least common multiple</a> is their product. This pops up in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Chinese Remainder Theorem\">Chinese Remainder Theorem</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_theory\" title=\"Number theory\">Number theory</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Greatest_common_divisor\" title=\"Greatest common divisor\">Greatest common divisor</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chicken_McNugget_Theorem\" title=\"Chicken McNugget Theorem\">Chicken McNugget Theorem</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018003427\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.021 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 113/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 289/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3147/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    7.388      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    7.388      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:1652:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018003427 and revision id 229844. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}